100:**`edge_text_an_unknown_name_inside_braces_is_never_printed` now has braces in it.** As first written it bound `known`, set it again inside a `try`, and asserted no failure — a body that never writes a brace pair, so it passed whether interpolation resolved an unknown name, refused it, or printed its braces as text. It could not be fixed the obvious way, either: a name nothing binds anywhere is an `AnalyzerError`, raised before the program runs, which `try` cannot catch — so `say "{nope}"` under `try` fails the whole file rather than the test. What *is* observable from Redblue is the same failure arriving at runtime, and the analyzer has exactly one place it reads a name the walk has not reached: a function body, analysed against the program as it is at the call. So the test declares `constant late` **below** the body that reads `{late}` and calls the body first. The analyzer sees a well formed `{late}`; the interpreter, running before that line, does not; `try` catches it, and `printed` is still `"untouched"` because the failure comes before the assignment. `edge_text_the_same_body_without_braces_is_not_a_failure` is the control — the identical body with the braces removed, where the name is part of the text and nothing is read at all, which is exactly the outcome a parser that printed its braces as text would give the test above. And `edge_the_corrected_redblue_test_still_exists_and_still_passes` now checks both that the test still exists and that its body still contains a brace pair.
